    title: 'Turf Care for Golf Communities | TIMELESS',
    description: 'Practice greens, chipping areas and clubhouse turf for Grand Strand golf communities, restored by someone who has built greens for 13 years.',
    h1: 'Turf care for golf communities and amenity greens',
    lede: `Prince Creek, Barefoot and Wachesaw were built around golf, and the backyard and clubhouse greens that came with them need more than a clean once a year. Brian has built synthetic greens for thirteen years through our sister company; this side of the business keeps the ones already in the ground rolling true.`,
    question: 'Do you maintain practice greens and clubhouse turf in golf communities?',
    answer: `Yes. TIMELESS Turf Restoration restores and maintains synthetic putting greens, chipping areas and clubhouse turf across Grand Strand golf communities including Prince Creek, Barefoot Resort and Wachesaw Plantation. Greens are cleared, brushed, top-dressed with kiln-dried green sand, rolled and checked for speed; clubhouse and common-area turf gets the same cleaning and odor treatment as any commercial lawn. Programs are quoted by square footage and scheduled around club and community events.`,
    takeaways: [
      'A backyard or clubhouse green loses speed and roll from matted debris and uneven sand depth, not from the turf wearing out.',
      'Golf communities like Prince Creek, Barefoot Resort and Wachesaw Plantation have more backyard greens per street than anywhere else on the coast, which makes a standing green-care schedule worth more than a reactive one.',
      'Clubhouse and amenity turf around a course takes foot traffic and cart proximity a private yard never sees, and needs its own line in the maintenance plan.',
      'Restoration is done by someone who also builds greens, which matters when the fix is more than a surface clean.',
    ],
    image: { src: '/assets/img/putting-green-at-sunset-wide.jpg', alt: 'Backyard putting green at sunset in a golf community' },
    diagram: 'green',
    sections: [
      { h2: 'Why greens in these communities need more than a clean', html: `<p>People buy in Prince Creek, Barefoot Resort or Wachesaw Plantation for the golf, and a lot of them put a synthetic green behind the house or the clubhouse invests in a practice green of its own. A green is not a lawn: speed comes from an even, correct depth of top-dress sand held in short fibers, and a true roll depends on that depth being consistent across the whole surface, not just clean. Debris that would only be an eyesore on a lawn, live-oak leaves, pine straw, pollen paste, changes how a ball rolls on a green, which is why greens need brushing and top-dressing on their own schedule, separate from the surrounding turf.</p>` },
      { h2: 'What we do on a green visit', html: `<p>Clear all debris first, then brush against the grain to lift the fibers and redistribute compacted sand. Clean the cups and the fringe, where debris collects and gets tracked back onto the putting surface. Top-dress with kiln-dried, rounded green sand to the correct depth, working it in evenly rather than dumping it in one spot. Roll the surface and check speed before calling it done. A green that gets this quarterly holds its roll; one that only gets it when someone notices the <a href="/turf-problems/putting-green-rolls-slow-or-bumpy/">ball is drifting</a> has usually lost a season of play in between.</p><ol><li>Clear all debris</li><li>Brush against the grain</li><li>Clean cups and fringe</li><li>Top-dress with kiln-dried green sand</li><li>Roll and check speed</li></ol>` },
      { h2: 'Clubhouse and amenity turf', html: `<p>The turf around a clubhouse, a cart staging area or a practice facility takes a different kind of abuse than a green: cart traffic, foot traffic before and after rounds, and the debris a course generates. It is cleaned and treated like any other commercial lawn, debris removal, <a href="/services/power-brushing/">power brushing</a>, rinse, and an antimicrobial pass where traffic is heaviest, on a schedule built around tee times and events rather than whenever it is convenient for us.</p>` },
      { h2: 'Scheduling around the club', html: `<p>A green cannot be closed during a member's round, and a clubhouse lawn cannot be wet during a wedding reception. We schedule green and amenity work for early mornings before the course opens, or for a maintenance window the pro shop already blocks off, and we coordinate directly with whoever runs that calendar rather than guessing at it.</p>` },
      { h2: 'Runoff and the golf-course context', html: `<p>Golf communities already manage water carefully, irrigation lakes, drainage swales, course-maintained turf, and our rinse water and treatment runoff follow the same stormwater rules as any other property in Horry or Georgetown County: nothing but stormwater to a drain, solids bagged, treatments left to dwell rather than flushed. We are used to working alongside a course's own grounds crew without duplicating or conflicting with what they already do.</p>` },
      { h2: 'What it costs', html: `<p>A backyard green is quoted by square footage the same way a lawn is, with putting-green restoration starting where our <a href="/pricing/">published bands</a> do; a clubhouse or amenity lawn follows the same commercial per-square-foot pricing as any other business turf. A standing quarterly green-care program, or a full-community contract covering clubhouse turf and member greens together, is quoted after we walk the property. Call before the spring restoration season if you want a green rolling true for the first warm weekend.</p>` },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'How often does a backyard putting green need professional care?', a: 'Quarterly is the realistic baseline: brush and check every visit, top-dress once a year, more often if the green sits under live oaks or pines that drop debris most of the year.' },
      { q: 'Can you tell if a green needs new sand or just a clean?', a: 'Yes, we check that on every visit. A green that has lost speed from debris and uneven sand gets cleared and top-dressed; a green with real fiber wear or backing damage is a repair conversation, and we say so rather than top-dressing over a problem.' },
      { q: 'Do you maintain clubhouse and amenity turf, not just greens?', a: 'Yes, the same cleaning and odor-control services we use on any commercial lawn, scheduled around the club calendar.' },
      { q: 'Since you build greens too, can you fix a green that is beyond cleaning?', a: 'If a green needs new fiber, a base repair or a full rebuild, that work goes through our sister company, TIMELESS Grass & Greens, and we will tell you honestly when a clean will not solve it.' },
      { q: 'Are you licensed and insured to work on community golf property?', a: 'Ask us and we will send what your community or club needs for its file.' },
    ],
    services: ['putting-green-restoration', 'power-brushing', 'artificial-turf-cleaning'],
    related: ['senior-living-communities', 'hoas-and-communities', 'installer-and-landscaper-partners'],
